# Every base image this repository builds from, pinned by digest, and every
# toolchain it installs, pinned by version and source hash. PLAN-014 M2
# (RFCT-108); the decision is PLAN-014's decision 4.
#
# WHY SELF-BUILT AND NOT `golang:` / `rust:`. PLAN-014 decision 4 rejected the
# official language images "in favour of base+toolchain self-builds for total
# consistency". The official images are a moving floor nobody here chose: they
# pick their own Debian release, their own CA bundle, their own git, and they
# change all three on their own schedule. mos already builds its container
# engine and its updater from source for exactly this reason -- see
# os/podman/versions.env and os/update/rauc/versions.env -- and a build
# environment is no less a shipped decision than the binaries it produces.
# What this costs is written down in PLAN-014's risks: toolchain security
# updates now arrive by editing this file. That is the trade, taken knowingly.
#
# WHY DIGESTS AND NOT TAGS. `debian:trixie-slim` is a name upstream re-points
# whenever it rebuilds; `debian:trixie-slim@sha256:...` is the bytes. A tag
# means the answer to "what did this build run on" is a date, and every build
# before that date is unreproducible without one. The digest makes the base a
# value in this file, which is the same thing os/podman/versions.env does for
# the engine's version and for the same reason: it has to be answerable from
# the tree rather than from a build log.
#
# THIS FILE IS THE UPGRADE INTERFACE. Change a value here, run the build, read
# what it says. Nothing else in os/build-env/ carries an image name or a
# version.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MECHANICS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# SOURCED, NOT PARSED. os/build-env/build.sh reads this with `.`, and the
# Dockerfiles read a comment-stripped derivative of it (`images.lock`) the same
# way. So `${...}` on the right-hand side expands, and a value must be safe to
# eval. Keep to plain assignments.
#
# PIN THE INDEX DIGEST, NOT A PER-ARCHITECTURE MANIFEST DIGEST. `docker buildx
# imagetools inspect debian:trixie-slim` prints both: one `Digest:` for the
# whole multi-architecture index, then one per platform. The index digest is
# the one to record. A per-architecture digest builds fine on the host that
# recorded it and then refuses every cross build with a platform error, which
# is a failure this project would hit on its FIRST arm64 build and not before
# -- the single alpine pin already in the tree
# (board/cx3576/rootfs/alpine/Dockerfile) is an index digest too, so this
# convention is the one already in use rather than a new one.
#
# HOW TO BUMP A DIGEST: set the digest to the literal string PENDING -- keeping
# the `name:tag@` part, so the tag being resolved is still recorded -- and run
# `make build-env`. The build resolves the tag, prints the digest it found, and
# FAILS. Paste it in and run again. Same shape as bumping a hash in
# os/podman/versions.env, and for the same reason: recording a digest is then
# an act rather than a copy from a page nobody re-checked.
#
# PENDING IS A HARD FAILURE HERE, WITH NO ENVIRONMENT VARIABLE THAT SOFTENS IT,
# and this differs from the two files it takes the pattern from. Both
# os/podman/Dockerfile and os/update/rauc/Dockerfile accept PENDING with a
# warning unless MOS_PODMAN_STRICT / MOS_RAUC_STRICT is 1, on the reasoning
# that "an unhashed source can reach a developer and never a release". Note
# that os/podman/versions.env's own prose already describes the behaviour this
# file implements -- "The build prints the hash it computed and fails" -- and
# that its Dockerfile does not do that by default; the prose is the contract
# worth keeping, so this file keeps it and drops the knob. The reason to drop
# it: an unpinned SOURCE fails a later hash check anyway, but an unpinned BASE
# IMAGE fails nothing at all downstream, so a warning here floats silently for
# as long as nobody reads the log. This file has one behaviour, in CI and on a
# laptop, and PENDING never survives a green build.
#
# WHAT A DIGEST PIN DOES NOT COVER, stated because the gap is easy to miss: it
# pins the base image's filesystem, NOT the apt archive that the build installs
# from on top of it. `apt-get install git` against a frozen debian:trixie-slim
# still resolves against live deb.debian.org and returns whatever trixie holds
# today. Closing that needs snapshot.debian.org (a dated archive URL, pinned
# here like anything else) and is not in RFCT-108's scope. Until then the floor
# minimums below are what stands between this build and a silent toolchain
# downgrade, which is why they are asserted rather than assumed, and the image
# records the versions it actually resolved (see os/build-env/base/Dockerfile).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NAMING, and the room left for the rest of M2
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   IMAGE_<distro>_<release>   an upstream base image, `name:tag@sha256:...`
#   <IMAGE>_FLOOR_<tool>_MIN   the minimum version a mos-build-<image> asserts
#   <IMAGE>_<tool>_VERSION     a toolchain tarball's version
#   <IMAGE>_<tool>_SHA256      that tarball's source hash
#
# The prefix before the first underscore is the CONSUMER, and that is
# load-bearing rather than decorative: os/build-env/build.sh gives each image a
# lock file filtered to its own prefix, so adding a pin for one image does not
# invalidate the cached layers of the others. os/podman/Dockerfile measured
# what the alternative costs -- one shared dependency list meant a one-package
# edit recompiled five unrelated components, about 42 minutes under emulation
# -- and the same arithmetic applies to a shared lock file.
#
# So M2b adds GO_/RUST_/C_ blocks and M2c adds IMAGE_ lines, and neither has to
# reshape anything above.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Base images
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The floor for everything mos builds itself. trixie is not a preference here:
# os/rootfs/Dockerfile.v2 assembles the device root from trixie, so a builder
# on any other release would link against a different glibc, libseccomp and
# libsubid than the one the binaries run on -- os/podman/Dockerfile records
# that constraint and the ten build iterations that established it.
#
# Recorded 2026-08-25. Upstream's own metadata dates this build 2026-08-24.
IMAGE_DEBIAN_TRIXIE=debian:trixie-slim@sha256:d7e12182ce18b85b93007c1dedf31f2d29e01ccf3182cc4017c709b6259bc132

# M2c adds the remaining bases here -- IMAGE_DEBIAN_BOOKWORM (the pack stage of
# os/rootfs/Dockerfile.v2), IMAGE_UBUNTU_2404 (the BSP builds under board/ and
# os/tests/handshake-test), IMAGE_ALPINE (already digest-pinned in place at
# board/cx3576/rootfs/alpine/Dockerfile, moving here) -- and rewires their
# Dockerfiles' FROM through them. Not pinned here yet, deliberately: a pin that
# no build reads is a value nothing can prove wrong.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# mos-build-base: the common floor
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The five packages every builder needs regardless of language, and nothing
# else. Per-language apt lists stay in their own Dockerfiles -- see the
# 42-minute measurement cited above; that is the same decision, applied to apt
# rather than to lock files.
#
# These are MINIMUMS, not pins, because the digest above does not pin the apt
# archive (see WHAT A DIGEST PIN DOES NOT COVER). What they catch is the
# direction that matters: a floor tool that went missing, or went BACKWARDS,
# which is what a base swap or a slimmed package looks like from inside the
# build. A trixie point release moving git forward passes, as it should.
#
# The values are trixie's, measured 2026-08-25 against the digest above:
# git 2.47.3, file 5.46, binutils 2.44, xz 5.8.1. Raise one only when trixie
# has actually moved, or the assertion stops meaning anything.
BASE_FLOOR_CODENAME=trixie
BASE_FLOOR_GIT_MIN=2.47
BASE_FLOOR_FILE_MIN=5.46
BASE_FLOOR_BINUTILS_MIN=2.44
BASE_FLOOR_XZ_MIN=5.8

# M2b adds the toolchain blocks -- GO_VERSION/GO_SHA256_<arch>,
# RUST_VERSION/RUST_SHA256_<arch>, and whatever C_ floor mos-build-c asserts --
# each pinned to a tarball hash the way os/podman/versions.env pins a source
# tree, and each asserted inside its own image the way BASE_FLOOR_* is asserted
# inside this one.
